<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lian Po</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lian Po was a leading general of the State of Zhao in the 3rd century BCE, known for victories over Qi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>. At Changping in 260 BCE he held a defensive line against Qin until Zhao replaced him with Zhao Kuo, whose army was destroyed. His reconciliation with the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-xiangru">Lin Xiangru</a>, when he came to apologise carrying thorns on his back, became a celebrated story.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lian-po.jpg" alt="Lian Po" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 馮夢龍 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%BB%89%E9%A0%97.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Lian Po, whose courtesy name was Hongye, served the state of Zhao in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>. The Thousand Character Classic names him with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bai-qi">Bai Qi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-jian-qin">Wang Jian</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-mu">Li Mu</a> as the four leading generals of the age. His early victories, which included wars against Qi and Wei, brought him fame and a long career in his home state.</p><p>He resented the minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lin-xiangru">Lin Xiangru</a> for his rapid rise. Lin went out of his way to avoid him, once turning his carriage aside rather than block the general's route. Lian Po took this for weakness until he learned that Lin gave way so that a private quarrel would not endanger the state. Their reconciliation gave rise to the chengyu Carrying Thorned Grass and Pleading Guilt, meaning a humble apology that asks for punishment and forgiveness.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At Changping he faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> army under Wang He and, unwilling to risk open battle, built a chain of forts that brought the invaders to a stalemate. Many courtiers of King Xiaocheng of Zhao, most of them heavily bribed by Qin agents, turned the king against this strategy, and he gave the command to Zhao Kuo, son of the general Zhao She. Zhao Kuo attacked in full strength, Qin replaced Wang He with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bai-qi">Bai Qi</a>, and the Zhao army walked into a trap; Zhao Kuo was killed and 400,000 Zhao soldiers were executed on Bai Qi's orders.</p><p>After Changping, Lian Po again led the army of Zhao and defeated an invasion by Yan. In his later years he lost the trust of the king of Zhao and fled first to Wei and then to Chu. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shouchun">Shouchun</a>, the Chu capital, having lived to see the decline of the state he had served.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lian Po” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Zhao produced famous ministers and generals, including Lin Xiangru, <b>Lian Po</b> and Li Mu.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-zhao">State of Zhao</a></li></ul></section>
